    "Singular Dev": "Alonso",
    "Solved Date": "2026-08-26 00:00:00",
    "Diagnostic": "On the Appointments page, when a scheduled appointment was missing its Room, Sales Associate, or Alterations Lead assignment, both the List and Calendar views displayed the text \"Missing Room\" / \"Missing Sales Associate\" / \"Missing Alterations Lead\" in an alert color. That wording implied a blocking data gap — but in practice, following a related fix applied days earlier, none of those three fields actually block Check In (only a missing Client does). The copy simply hadn't been updated to reflect that behavior change, creating the impression that reception couldn't check a client in without those fields completed, when in reality they already could.",
    "Solution": "The wording was changed from \"Missing\" to \"Must Assign\" for the three non-blocking fields (Room, Sales Associate, Alterations Lead), with the treatment differentiated by view to stay consistent with each one's own design: in the List view, where each column already carries its own header, the text reads simply \"Must Assign\" (no field name repeated, avoiding redundancy especially for the longer labels); in the Calendar view, where there are no column headers, the field name was kept in the same neutral typographic style already used when the field has a value (e.g. \"Room: \"), with only the word \"Must Assign\" highlighted in the alert color — matching the \"Field: value\" pattern that view already follows. The \"Missing Client\" text (the one case that genuinely blocks check-in) was left completely untouched. All representative test cases were run — every affected field, in every possible combination of empty/filled state, across both views — confirming the implementation behaves correctly in each one.",
